I finally got tired of trying to keep my mail archives in a sane manner
using the standard 4.2 folder mechanisms in mail. I get a fair amount of
mail (he says with a straight face) and I find I'm constantly looking for
something that came in three days ago. After trying to wade through a 1.5
megabyte folder for the third time in three hours after something, I hacked
this up.

Arcmail will parse out the messages in a Berkeley (4.2, probably 4.1)
mailbox format file and store them in a given subdirectory in the format:

    <archive_dir>/<Month><day>/<account_name>

    where Month is {Jan-Dec}, day is the day is was sent or received, and
    account name is the account that sent it or that you sent it to.
    Arcmail is smart enough to use the address in the To: line if the From:
    line is you (or whatever is defined by USER). Acrmail strips out all of
    the header garbage except From, From:, To: and Subject, thereby
    shrinking your archive further.

    This is a 'quick hack' so there isn't a man page (yet) and there are a
    few compiled in constants that could be found in the environment-- they
    are actually isolated at the top of the program, though. Any comments
    and bugs are always welcome...

    chuq

